{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/cricket-for-beginners-how-the-game-works/audio/main-d7878523839c.mp3","durationSec":179.904,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":2.34,"text":"This simple guide explains cricket for beginners."},{"start":3.46,"end":4.78,"text":"A cricket match has two teams."},{"start":5.2,"end":6.68,"text":"Each team has eleven players."},{"start":7.34,"end":8.14,"text":"One team bats."},{"start":8.76,"end":9.7,"text":"The other team fields."},{"start":11.36,"end":13.18,"text":"The main action happens on the pitch."},{"start":13.7,"end":16.08,"text":"It is a long area in the centre."},{"start":16.08,"end":18.6,"text":"There is one wicket at each end of the pitch."},{"start":19.36,"end":21.38,"text":"A wicket has three wooden sticks."},{"start":22.32,"end":23.96,"text":"These sticks are called stumps."},{"start":24.76,"end":27.16,"text":"Two small wooden pieces sit on top."},{"start":27.68,"end":29.62,"text":"They are called bails."},{"start":29.62,"end":31.78,"text":"The wickets are important targets in the game."},{"start":32.36,"end":33.62,"text":"The batter uses a bat."},{"start":34.1,"end":36.56,"text":"The batter tries to hit the ball and score runs."},{"start":37.22,"end":38.64,"text":"Two batters are on the pitch."},{"start":39.18,"end":40.84,"text":"They stand at opposite ends."},{"start":41.48,"end":43.72,"text":"The bowler bowls the ball toward the batter and wicket."},{"start":44.24,"end":47.36,"text":"The bowler tries to stop runs or get the batter out."},{"start":48.46,"end":50.76,"text":"The wicketkeeper stands behind the wicket."},{"start":51.42,"end":53.66,"text":"This player catches or stops the ball."},{"start":54.32,"end":56.04,"text":"The other players are fielders."},{"start":56.48,"end":58,"text":"They stand around the field."},{"start":58,"end":60.94,"text":"They catch the ball, stop it, and throw it back."},{"start":60.94,"end":63.18,"text":"Runs are the points in cricket."},{"start":63.74,"end":67.08,"text":"For one run, the two batters run and safely change ends."},{"start":67.56,"end":69.18,"text":"A batter can also score a boundary."},{"start":69.94,"end":71.64,"text":"The edge of the field is the boundary."},{"start":72.28,"end":75.44,"text":"A ball that reaches it after touching the ground scores four runs."},{"start":76.02,"end":79.46,"text":"A ball that goes over it without touching the ground scores six runs."},{"start":80.12,"end":80.98,"text":"Here is an example."},{"start":81.76,"end":84.92,"text":"Team Blue scores one run, then four, then six."},{"start":85.2,"end":86.78,"text":"The total is eleven."},{"start":86.78,"end":90.1,"text":"The scoreboard can show “Team Blue 11/0.”"},{"start":90.1,"end":92.38,"text":"This means eleven runs and no batters out."},{"start":93.64,"end":95.04,"text":"The bowler does not bowl forever."},{"start":95.04,"end":97.9,"text":"Cricket divides the bowling into short groups."},{"start":98.36,"end":100.1,"text":"One group is called an over."},{"start":100.7,"end":102.7,"text":"An over has six valid balls."},{"start":103.1,"end":106.18,"text":"A valid ball is a delivery that counts in the game."},{"start":106.82,"end":109,"text":"The bowler bowls the first valid ball."},{"start":109.4,"end":110.72,"text":"Then the second ball counts."},{"start":111.2,"end":114.12,"text":"This continues until the sixth valid ball."},{"start":114.12,"end":115.78,"text":"The over is then complete."},{"start":115.78,"end":117.86,"text":"Overs help players count the game."},{"start":118.24,"end":121.66,"text":"For example, three complete overs contain eighteen valid balls."},{"start":122.56,"end":125.12,"text":"When a batter is out, that batter’s turn ends."},{"start":125.58,"end":127.24,"text":"Another batter usually comes to the pitch."},{"start":127.96,"end":129.74,"text":"There are several ways to get a batter out."},{"start":130.06,"end":131.24,"text":"Here are three common ways."},{"start":131.24,"end":134.68,"text":"**Bowled:** The bowler’s ball hits and breaks the wicket."},{"start":135.54,"end":137.06,"text":"**Caught:** The batter hits the ball."},{"start":137.42,"end":139.58,"text":"A fielder catches it before it touches the ground."},{"start":140.46,"end":143.96,"text":"**Run out:** A batter is outside the safe area near the wicket."},{"start":144.58,"end":147.2,"text":"A fielder uses the ball to break that wicket fairly."},{"start":147.2,"end":151.16,"text":"Cricket has three main international formats."},{"start":151.52,"end":153.56,"text":"An innings is one team’s turn to bat."},{"start":154.12,"end":156.32,"text":"A Test match can last five days."},{"start":156.86,"end":158.28,"text":"Each team can have two innings."},{"start":159.2,"end":160.66,"text":"Test cricket is the longest format."},{"start":161.06,"end":162.48,"text":"An ODI is a One Day International."},{"start":162.98,"end":164.18,"text":"Each team has 50 overs."},{"start":164.64,"end":167.06,"text":"A T20 International has 20 overs for each team."},{"start":167.42,"end":169.04,"text":"It usually takes about three hours."},{"start":169.04,"end":171.52,"text":"It is the shortest of these three formats."},{"start":171.52,"end":173.3,"text":"The basic ideas stay the same."},{"start":174,"end":179.52,"text":"Teams bat, bowl, field, score runs, and get batters out."}],"words":[{"text":"This","start":0,"end":0.16},{"text":"simple","start":0.16,"end":0.54},{"text":"guide","start":0.54,"end":0.86},{"text":"explains","start":0.86,"end":1.38},{"text":"cricket","start":1.38,"end":1.66},{"text":"for","start":1.66,"end":1.96},{"text":"beginners","start":1.96,"end":2.34},{"text":"A","start":3.46,"end":3.48},{"text":"cricket","start":3.48,"end":3.7},{"text":"match","start":3.7,"end":3.94},{"text":"has","start":3.94,"end":4.12},{"text":"two","start":4.12,"end":4.4},{"text":"teams","start":4.4,"end":4.78},{"text":"Each","start":5.2,"end":5.36},{"text":"team","start":5.36,"end":5.64},{"text":"has","start":5.64,"end":5.84},{"text":"eleven